module Auth.Simple.Proof exposing ( ResolvedUser, Authenticated, Outcome(..), resolvedUserId, userId )

{-| The witness types that keep a hand-rolled authentication pipeline honest.

An identifier module's `resolve` returns a [`ResolvedUser`](#ResolvedUser); a
credential module's `check` consumes it and, on success, returns an
[`Authenticated`](#Authenticated) inside an [`Outcome`](#Outcome); token
issuance (e.g. `Auth.Simple.Token.Session.issue`) demands the
[`Authenticated`](#Authenticated). Neither type can be constructed outside this
package, so a pipeline that skips a step — issuing a session for a user that
was merely *resolved*, never *authenticated* — does not compile.

You can read the user id out of either witness ([`resolvedUserId`](#resolvedUserId),
[`userId`](#userId)); extraction is harmless, minting is what's gated.

@docs ResolvedUser, Authenticated, Outcome, resolvedUserId, userId

-}

import Auth.Simple.Internal.Proof as Internal


{-| Proof that an identifier (a username, an email, …) resolved to an existing
user. Produced by identifier modules — e.g.
`Auth.Simple.Identifier.Username.resolve` — and consumed by credential checks.
Holding one means "this account exists", nothing more: it is not permission to
issue a token.
-}
type alias ResolvedUser =
    Internal.ResolvedUser


{-| Proof that a credential check accepted a candidate secret for a user.
Produced only by credential modules — e.g.
`Auth.Simple.Credential.Password.check` — inside an [`Outcome`](#Outcome), and
demanded by token issuance. If you hold one, the user proved who they are.
-}
type alias Authenticated =
    Internal.Authenticated


{-| The outcome of a credential check. An expected negative — the secret didn't
match, or no user owned the identifier (`Rejected`), or the credential is
temporarily locked after repeated failures (`Locked`) — is a value, not an
error; a check's error type is reserved for real faults (database, crypto).

`Rejected` deliberately covers both "unknown identifier" and "wrong secret", so
a caller (or an attacker reading its responses) cannot tell whether an account
exists. `Locked` is distinct: it is only reachable after repeated observable
failures against an identifier that therefore is known to exist, and a
locked-out user with the *correct* secret deserves a better answer than a shrug.
-}
type Outcome
    = Accepted Authenticated
    | Rejected
    | Locked


{-| The id of the user an identifier resolved to.
-}
resolvedUserId : ResolvedUser -> String
resolvedUserId (Internal.ResolvedUser record) =
    record.userId


{-| The id of the user a credential check authenticated.
-}
userId : Authenticated -> String
userId (Internal.Authenticated record) =
    record.userId
